  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1236 PM CDT Thu May 28 2026\n\nAn upper-level trough should become negatively tilted over the Four \nCorners region by Friday night. This will place the region in an \nactive southwest flow aloft through Sunday. A warm and moist low \nlevel airmass will also persist with dewpoints in the upper 60s. For \nnow, there is low (<30%) chance for thunderstorms Friday evening. \nStorms should be fairly scattered in nature and initiate along a \ndryline in the Permian Basin then push into our western counties. \nSPC has a Marginal Risk of severe storms across our western counties \nfor Friday, with strong wind gusts and hail being the main concerns. \nA similar setup is expected for Saturday and Sunday.  As a result, \nadditional rounds of thunderstorms are expected for Saturday night \nand Sunday night with otherwise near-normal temperatures.\n\nThe main upper-level trough over the western US should lift to the \nnorthern Plains starting Monday. The pattern will become a bit murky \nfor the first half of next week. While models show some ridging \nbuilding in across Texas, they also show a weak upper low setting up \nacross the state.  Models also show a \"back-door\" frontal boundary \nmoving through central Texas Monday through Wednesday. This would \nlead to more chances for showers and thunderstorms during this time. \nHowever, given the uncertainty of timing and location of these key \nfeatures, overall rain chances were kept low as storms should be \nscattered in nature.",
